<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>The best time to bring in a public adjuster is early, before the first inspection, but help is often still possible after an estimate, a payment, or even a denial.</li>
<li>The real limits are deadlines: policy conditions, recoverable depreciation windows, appraisal timelines, and contractual deadlines to file suit.</li>
<li>Many Texas property policies set a deadline to sue, often two years and one day from when the claim accrued. Check your policy and talk with a Texas attorney.</li>
<li>Weather-related lawsuits generally require written notice at least 61 days before filing under Insurance Code Chapter 542A. That is attorney territory, not public adjuster territory.</li>
<li>If you signed a release or the claim is in litigation, have an attorney review your options before hiring anyone else.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>People often call a public adjuster with the same worried question: “Did I wait too long?” Maybe the insurer already sent a check that barely covers the shingles. Maybe a denial letter arrived. Maybe a ceiling stain showed up months after the spring hail. So <strong>when is it too late to hire a public adjuster?</strong> The honest answer is that there is no single cutoff. It depends on where your claim stands and which deadlines apply. This article walks through each stage of a Texas property claim and what a public adjuster can, and cannot, still do.</p>

<p>One important note up front: public adjusters are not attorneys. When a question involves a lawsuit, a deadline to sue, or a pre-suit notice, the right person to ask is a Texas attorney. We will point out where those lines are.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/claim-evidence-organized-desk.webp" alt="Adjuster organizing printed claim photos and notes on a desk" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>

<h2>The short answer: earlier is better, but later is often still possible</h2>

<p>The earlier a public adjuster is involved, the more of the record they help create: the first photos, the first estimate, the first conversations with the insurer’s adjuster. Every later stage builds on that record. Coming in late means working with whatever has already been said and submitted.</p>

<p>But “late” is not the same as “too late.” Many claims are underpaid or partly denied after the insurer’s first inspection, and that is exactly when a lot of San Marcos property owners first look for help. What actually closes the door is usually one of these:</p>
<ul>
<li>A policy deadline passes, such as a window to submit a proof of loss or to complete repairs and collect recoverable depreciation.</li>
<li>You sign a release or settlement agreement that resolves the claim.</li>
<li>A contractual or legal deadline to file suit runs out.</li>
<li>The claim moves into litigation, where an attorney takes the lead.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Stage by stage: what a public adjuster can still do</h2>

<h3>Before you file the claim</h3>
<p>This is the ideal time. A public adjuster can review your policy, help you understand deductibles and exclusions, and document the damage before the insurer’s inspection. You should still report the loss promptly. Most policies require prompt notice, and waiting can hurt your claim. Bringing in help and reporting the claim can happen in the same week.</p>

<h3>After filing, before the insurer’s inspection</h3>
<p>Still an excellent time. Your public adjuster can be present at the inspection, point out damage that is easy to miss (soft metal dents, cracked tiles, interior moisture), and make sure the insurer’s adjuster sees the full scope.</p>

<h3>After the inspection, before the estimate</h3>
<p>Help is still very practical here. A public adjuster can prepare an independent estimate and documentation to submit while the insurer is still evaluating the claim.</p>

<h3>After the insurer’s estimate or first payment</h3>
<p>This is the most common time people reach out. If the estimate misses rooms, roof components, or realistic local pricing, a public adjuster can review it line by line and submit a supplement or request reconsideration. Watch two things closely: whether you signed anything described as a release or final settlement, and your policy’s deadline for completing repairs and claiming recoverable depreciation. Our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains that holdback.</p>

<h3>After a full or partial denial</h3>
<p>A denial letter should state the reason. A public adjuster can compare that reason with your policy language and the physical evidence, and in some cases request reconsideration with new documentation, such as photos of hidden damage or weather data for the date of loss. If the denial turns on a legal interpretation, or a deadline to sue is getting close, talk with a Texas attorney. Our guide <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-claim-was-denied-in-san-marcos/">What to Do If Your Claim Was Denied in San Marcos</a> covers the steps.</p>

[[cta]]

<h3>After the claim is closed</h3>
<p>A closed claim is not always a finished one. Insurers may reopen a claim when new, related damage is found, for example roof decking that was rotted under shingles, or an interior leak that traces back to the same storm. Whether that is possible depends on the policy, the facts, any release you signed, and how much time has passed. A public adjuster can help assess whether there is a supportable basis to ask.</p>

<h3>Supplements during repairs</h3>
<p>Repairs often uncover damage no one could see during the first inspection. Supplements are a normal part of many claims, and a public adjuster can document and submit them as they come up. Our <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements/">hail damage claim supplements</a> article explains how they typically work.</p>

<h3>When appraisal has been invoked</h3>
<p>If you and the insurer agree the damage is covered but disagree on the amount, many policies let either side demand appraisal. Each side picks an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and an award agreed to by any two is generally binding on the amount (your policy language controls). Appraisal decides amount, not coverage. Many policies require each appraiser to be “competent and disinterested,” so read that wording when choosing yours. A public adjuster can still help prepare the documentation behind the process. See our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page.</p>

<h3>When a lawsuit is being considered or filed</h3>
<p>At this point, a Texas attorney should be leading. Two rules come up often:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Contractual deadlines to sue.</strong> Many Texas property policies limit the time to file suit, often to two years and one day from when the claim accrued, as allowed under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/CP/htm/CP.16.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Civil Practice and Remedies Code §16.070</a>. Your policy wording matters, and so does how a court would measure “accrued.” That is a legal question.</li>
<li><strong>Chapter 542A pre-suit notice.</strong> For weather-related property claims, <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542A.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 542A</a> generally requires written notice to the insurer at least 61 days before filing suit. An attorney should prepare and send it.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are anywhere near these deadlines, call an attorney first. A public adjuster cannot extend a legal deadline or give legal advice about one.</p>

