<h2>Insurer deadlines that work in your favor</h2>

<p>Timing is not only about your deadlines. Texas’s <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prompt Payment of Claims law</a> sets deadlines for insurers, too. In general, an insurer has 15 days after notice of a claim to acknowledge it, begin its investigation, and request the information it needs. After receiving everything it asked for, it generally has 15 business days to accept or reject the claim, or it can notify you that it needs more time, up to 45 more days. Once it accepts, payment is generally due within 5 business days. After a weather catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the commissioner, these deadlines can be extended by 15 days. Surplus lines insurers can have different timeframes.</p>

<p>If your claim seems stuck, a public adjuster can help keep a dated record of every request and response. Our <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">article on delayed claims</a> and <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> page go further.</p>

<h2>Late-discovered damage in San Marcos</h2>

<p>Some damage simply takes time to show itself. A hail-bruised roof may not leak until the next heavy rain. A small supply-line leak behind a cabinet may not stain the ceiling for weeks. In Hays County, where NOAA records show hail peaking in April and May and flash flooding peaking in June, May, and October, it is common for a fall storm to reveal damage from spring.</p>

<p>If you find damage you believe came from an earlier storm, report it to your insurer promptly, document it carefully, and note the date you discovered it. Being able to point to a specific storm helps. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page and the NOAA records behind it can help you identify likely dates. For example, NOAA lists 3-inch hail at San Marcos Academy west of the city on May 9, 2024 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>).</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/appraisal-dispute-review-table.webp" alt="Professionals at a table reviewing roof damage photos with a gavel and scales nearby" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Later-stage disputes, such as appraisal or pre-suit questions, involve stricter procedures and, often, an attorney.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What to do right now if you think you are running late</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Find your policy.</strong> Read the conditions section for notice, proof of loss, appraisal, and “suit against us” language.</li>
<li><strong>Gather your claim file.</strong> Estimates, letters, emails, payment records, and photos. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> helps.</li>
<li><strong>Do not sign a release</strong> you do not fully understand.</li>
<li><strong>Note every date:</strong> date of loss, date reported, inspection date, payment dates, and denial date.</li>
<li><strong>Get a review.</strong> A public adjuster can tell you whether there is room to supplement or request reconsideration. An attorney can tell you about legal deadlines.</li>
</ol>

